 "title": "Use Color Palettes for Web Design",
 "topic": "design",
 "summary": "This video introduces a simple 60/30/10 rule for choosing website color palettes. By using two primary colors for 90% of your design and a contrasting, 'weird' accent color for your call-to-action buttons, you can create more professional-looking and effective websites.",
 "key_points": [
  "Follow the 60/30/10 rule: use two colors for 90% of the design, and a third, contrasting color for the remaining 10%.",
  "Apply the accent color only to the specific button you want users to click.",
  "Pairing black and white with lime green creates a 'startup' vibe.",
  "Pairing navy and cream with coral makes a design feel classy and clickable.",
  "Pairing forest green and beige with electric purple helps a design look like a brand."
 ],
 "content": "the website color palette trick nobody tells you: one unexpected accent color\n\nblack and white plus lime, navy and cream plus coral, forest green and beige plus electric purple. two colors do 90% of the work, the weird one only goes on the button you want pressed\n\nsave this for your next site. which combo would you actually ship?\n\n#colortheory #webdesign #uidesign #colorpalette #design\n\nTranscript:\n\"Black and white looks fine. Now add lime green and it looks like a startup. Navy and cream, classy. Throw in some coral, now people actually click. Forest green and beige, calm. Add one hit of electric purple, now it is a brand. The trick: two colors do 90% of the work, one weird color gets 10% and only on the button you want to be pressed. Follow for more.\"\n\nText on screen:\n- Midnight Black #0B0B0C\n- Pure White #FFFFFF\n- Lime Green #C5F432\n- Navy #14213D\n- Cream #F4EEDD\n- Coral #FF985B\n- Forest Green #1F302B\n- Sand Beige #EEDCC4\n- Electric Purple #8B5CF8\n- 60/30/10\n- Diego A. Does",
